/**
\page modules Yoyo modules

Yoyo is devided into individual modules with as small coupling between the modules as possible.

The modules contain an interface each and possibly some package-private classes and functions.

\section modules_sdk Yoyo-Sdk

This is the most basic of the Yoyo modules. It contains the minimal interface necessary to create
plugins for Yoyo. All other modules (except for \ref modules_command) depend on it.

This module is heavily documented. This documentation, the header files and an CMake export module
are packaged on the install target to be shipped alongside the Yoyo binaries to any customer.

The content of this module is:
- The most basic node for the tree like data model
- derived classes from this basic node for
    - Gui nodes
    - Data nodes
    - Protocol nodes
    - Communication nodes
- a class for handling the documentation of nodes
- the node factory
- and the base class for Yoyo plugins

\section modules_command Command framework

The command framework provides two classes:
- the command
- the command handler
- some helper objects

The command represents an undoable and re-doable command atomic action.

The command handler manages the commands in an undo stack.

In contrast to the usual design pattern, Yoyo doesn't use inheritance to specialize commands, but
dependency injection with std::function.

Even though technically a singleton, the main command handler is stored as a package private object
and can be accessed using free functions. This allows different instances, if necessary.

\section modules_io File Input and Output

Yoyo comes with an io framework. This framework is independent of the file format, and even
independent of files (using std-streams to write/read).

The interface defines:
- a serializer
- a deserializer
- a manager class

The manager class holds a list of serializers and deserializers for different formats.

Even though technically a singleton, the main io-manager is stored as a package private object and
can be accessed using free functions. This allows different instances, if necessary.

Currently IO objects are available for json file format.

\todo discuss whether to allow IO objects into plugins.

\section modules_plugin Plugin manager

Since Yoyo provides a plugin framework, it also needs to load these plugins. It was decided to have
an aproach which is a bit more comfortable: The plugins are not only loaded from a single predefined
directory (mostly the plugin directory inside the installation folder), but they are defined by a
description file.

With the plugin description, the plugins can be located anywhere on the disk. The known plugins can
be displayed in the Gui and plugins can be loaded and unloaded at runtime. From session to session
the plugin manager remembers the known plugin and their load state and sets loads plugins at startup
accordingly.

This module contains the "backend" management, not the Gui (\ref modules_gui_plugin).

The plugin manager itself is the interface of this module. Package private components include the
settings management and deserialization for the plugin description.

\section modules_model Data model

The data model contains anything "under-the-hood". Stuff which Yoyo provides, but which is not
related directly to Gui.

\subsection modules_model_connection Connectivity

This module is the most important module for Yoyo functionality. It provides the connectivity
between data nodes and non-data nodes. It watches connection properties and connects the node of
that name, if it exists. In addition it also connects other "connected" properties.

\note This module is still a bit messy.

\subsection modules_model_nodes Predefined nodes

In this module, there are some nodes, which are needed for a basic functioning of Yoyo. There is a
set of data nodes (hopefully complete) and some basic Gui nodes, like line-edit, combo-box, check-
box, etc.

In addition, organizational nodes are defined here:
- configuration, the root node
- data root, the root for all data nodes
- gui root, the root node for all gui nodes

Currently it is not planned to add some default protocols or communication nodes, but this is not
fixed.

\todo there are still nodes missing: something like configuration_data, protocol-root,
communication-root, etc.

The interface of this module consists of factory populating free functions and the declaration of
the configuration class. The rest is package private.

\section modules_engine Engines

\subsection modules_engine_js JavaScript Engine
\todo JavaScript Engine: to be implemented.
\subsection modules_engine_other Other engines?
\todo other engines: to be defined

\section modules_gui Graphical User Interface

In the gui directory Yoyo collects individual widgets, which can be used by the main gui. This does
not include gui nodes, though. The widgets defined here are organizational, meant for editing or
displaying information about the data model.

Each one of the Gui modules defines an interface widget using the pimpl idiom. All internals are
hidden from the rest of Yoyo to enforce low coupling, one of the basic principles of object-
oriented software engineering.

\subsection modules_gui_tree Tree Widget

This widget displays the complete data model as a tree. Drag and Drop is supported within the widget
and for data nodes, from this widget to the main display. Nodes from the data model can be selected,
which will display their properties in the \ref modules_gui_property.

This module and the IO module are the only modules, which are allowed to create nodes. In case of
the Tree Widget, the creation and destruction of nodes is undoable.

\todo The current implementation of the undo for add and remove might introduce a memory leak,
    because the lambdas capture the shared-pointers of the nodes, so they are only really destroyed,
    when the undo-stack is cleared. Solution could be to keep a property-value-list for each
    destroyed node, but memory wise, this should not make that much of a difference, because the
    property-value-lists then are captured.

Being a pretty featuresome module, the actual functionality was split up into several classes (the S
in SOLID principles). One to handle the context menu, type dependent adapters to handle what data
to display and the model for Drag&Drop handling as well as interface to the tree-view.

The interface contains solely of the widget itself. The model and other functional parts are package
private.

\subsection modules_gui_tool Tool Widget

The Tool Widget displays a list of available gui nodes, which can be dragged and dropped on the main
display to add new items.

This widget is pretty simple compared to other widgets, it only contains the item model as
controller for the data received from the respective node factory (MVC, Model-View-Controller
pattern: Model is the installed nodes data from the factory, View is the list-view).

The interface contains solely of the widget itself. The model and other functional parts are package
private.

\subsection modules_gui_property Property Widget

The Property Widget displays and allows to modify the properties of the selected node.

The widget itself has a rather small implementation, the handling of the individual property types
is delegated to sub widgets. All internally used widgets are package private.

This module is by far the biggest of the gui modules. This is not due to complexity, but due to the
fact, that there are so many possible property types to handle (\ref properties_extend).

The Property Widget itself requests the creation of individual property input widgets, depending on
the respective property type; one for each property. These input widgets are package private, so not
accessible from outside other than by iterating through the Qt parent/child tree.

There is not a one-to-one relationship of input-widgets to property types, since some widgets can
handle several similar types. The idea had been to have many small, simple classes.

A generic approach using templates was not possible, since the Meta Object Compiler does
not support templates. And the property management by Yoyo (the actual type of the node being
unknown or even inaccessible to the Property Widget) heavily relies on the Qt property system.

The interface contains solely of the widget itself.

\subsection modules_gui_plugin Plugin management

This is the Gui for the \ref modules_plugin described earlier. It displays known plugins, requests
the load of plugins on user request.

\todo Implement gui::plugin_dialog.

The interface contains solely of the widget itself.

\subsection modules_gui_help Help widget

The user help is written with doxygen and can be found in this repository in doc/user/. The
generated html files along with a css are deployed with the installation. The purpose of the Help
Widget is to enable the user to view the help from within Yoyo.

\todo Implement gui::help_widget.

The interface contains solely of the widget itself.

\subsection modules_gui_log Log widget

Yoyo internals does some logging to dedicated logging contexts. The user can use the Log Widget to
select one or more logging contexts and display what has been logged during the current session.

\todo Implement gui::log_widget.

The interface contains solely of the widget itself.

\section modules_yoyo The Yoyo application

This module is less a module, but the final executable. Here all other modules come together to form
the Yoyo application.

The Yoyo application contains of a main window, owning the individual widgets defined in the gui
modules and handling their visibility through menu actions.

It also contains an application class, which is the owner of the data-model. It owns not only the
node factories and the \ref modules_plugin, but also the defined \ref modules_engine and the
\ref modules_io. The file_management object is responsible for handling user request for loading and
storing.
*/
